 dict(pmid='43055042', doi='10.1016/j.envadv.2026.100750', journal='Environ Adv', title='Associations between ambient air pollutants and chronic pain prevalence in US children, 2023: A cross-sectional, national study.', tier='C', basis='Not stated', short='NSCH 2023, 54,880 US children: chronic pain associated with SO2 only (aOR 1.08); PM2.5, PM10, PM10-2.5 null at state level', design='Cross-sectional survey', pm='State-level EPA estimates of PM2.5, PM10, PM10-2.5 plus O3, SO2, NO2, NO', geo='USA', endpoint='Parent-reported chronic or frequent pain, past 12 months', affil='Stanford University, USA; Stanford University, USA', n="54,880 children aged 0-17 from the 2023 National Survey of Children's Health linked to state-level EPA estimates of seven pollutants including PM2.5, PM10 and PM10-2.5; survey-weighted logistic regression adjusted for age, sex, income and asthma, with FDR correction and PCA mixture scores. Chronic pain prevalence was 5.1%. Only SO2 survived correction (aOR 1.08, 95% CI 1.02-1.13); the PM fractions were not associated and mixture scores were null. Weakness: every child in a state shares one exposure value, leaving about 51 exposure units; the SO2 increment is not given; cross-sectional with parent report. Relevance: a PM null at this spatial resolution says little - it is the scale problem neighbourhood-level sensing is meant to fix.", sub=corpus.OTHR),
 dict(pmid='43053703', doi='10.1080/15287394.2026.2764229', journal='J Toxicol Environ Health A', title='Association between fine particulate air pollution and the risk of death from kidney cancer in Taiwan.', tier='C', basis='Not stated', short='Taiwan 66 municipalities 2010-2023 ecological: higher PM2.5 tertile with lower kidney-cancer mortality in men; null in women', design='Observational - ecological', pm='Municipal annual PM2.5 in tertiles', geo='Taiwan', endpoint='Age-standardised kidney-cancer mortality', affil='I-Shou University, Taiwan; Kaohsiung Medical University, Taiwan', n='Ecological study of 66 Taiwanese municipalities, 2010-2023: annual PM2.5 tertiles against age-standardised kidney-cancer mortality, adjusted for lung-cancer mortality as a smoking proxy, urbanisation, household income and physician density. Men in higher-PM2.5 municipalities had significantly lower kidney-cancer mortality; women showed no association. The abstract gives no RRs or CIs, and the authors allow chance. Weakness: ecological, no latency, and adjusting for lung-cancer mortality - itself partly caused by PM2.5 - can over-adjust and reverse signs; diagnostic access varies with urbanisation. Relevance: low; an instructive case of proxy-adjustment bias rather than evidence on PM2.5 and renal cancer.', sub=corpus.OTHR),
 dict(pmid='43052908', doi='10.1186/s12889-026-29087-1', journal='BMC Public Health', title='Development of metabolic syndrome identification model by integrating Korea national health and nutrition examination survey data with regional particulate matter, air pollutants, and green environmental factors.', tier='C', basis='Not stated', short='KNHANES 20,035 adults: metabolic-syndrome classifiers with regional air pollution reach AUC 0.64-0.67; demographics dominate', design='Cross-sectional (machine learning)', pm='Regional average air pollution and green space from national statistics, assigned by residence region', geo='Republic of Korea', endpoint='Metabolic syndrome classification', affil='aSSIST University, Republic of Korea; aSSIST University, Republic of Korea', n='20,035 KNHANES adults aged 30+ (2018-2022) linked to regional average air pollution and green-space data; eight classifiers (SVM, logistic regression, random forest, XGBoost, LightGBM and three MLPs) with class weighting for imbalance. AUCs ranged 0.644-0.666; DeepMLP was highest but not significantly better than the tree ensembles by DeLong and bootstrap tests. SHAP and permutation importance ranked sex, age and education highest; the abstract gives no PM-specific contribution. Weakness: region-level exposure assigned to individuals, cross-sectional prediction rather than inference, modest discrimination; importance scores are not effects. Relevance: low; adding coarse regional PM to a classifier adds little, as expected when exposure contrast is averaged away.', sub=corpus.CVM),
 dict(pmid='43057008', doi='10.1016/j.ecoenv.2026.121071', journal='Ecotoxicol Environ Saf', title='Subchronic 1-nitropyrene exposure induces meibomian gland dysfunction via lipid peroxidation-mediated ferroptosis.', tier='C', basis='Lab / research instrument', short='Topical 1-nitropyrene in mice causes meibomian gland dysfunction via ferroptosis; ferrostatin-1 rescues, PPARalpha modulates', design='Animal exposure study + in vitro', pm='Topical ocular 1-nitropyrene 10 mg/mL for 14 days in mice; human meibomian gland epithelial cells in vitro', geo='China (laboratory)', endpoint='Meibomian gland dysfunction, ferroptosis and lipid peroxidation markers', affil='Second Affiliated Hospital of Anhui Medical University, China; Second Affiliated Hospital of Anhui Medical University, China', n='Mice received topical ocular 1-nitropyrene, a nitro-PAH constituent of diesel-derived fine particles, at 10 mg/mL for 14 days. Exposure produced meibomian gland dysfunction - duct obstruction, acinar atrophy, reduced tear secretion, corneal epithelial defects - with loss of progenitor markers (p63, Lrig1), increased keratinisation (K10), iron overload (ferritin, NCOA4) and lipid peroxidation (GSH down, MDA up). Ferrostatin-1 abolished ferroptosis and MGD; in human meibomian gland epithelial cells PPARalpha agonism and knockdown modulated 1-NP-induced ferroptosis. Weakness: a pure compound at a concentration far above plausible ocular deposition of ambient 1-NP, delivered as drops rather than particles. Relevance: low for sensing; mechanistic plausibility for ocular-surface associations reported with traffic PM.', sub=corpus.MECH),
